  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1203 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nSynoptic flow over the next week will largely be dominated by \ntroughing currently located over Washington/southern British \nColumbia and its slow movement through the great basin and how \nit interacts with downstream eastern CONUS riding. In the near \nterm the post-tropical remnants of Isaias will work to weaken \nsubtropical high over Bermuda allowing eastern ridging an avenue\nto weaken and shift east/southeast. At the same time troughing \nacross eastern canada/Hudson bay will work south flattening the \nriding and allowing flow across the north CONUS to become more \nzonal by early next week in the mid levels.\n\n- Today\n\nLooking at current surface obs a cold front is currently \nsituated across central North Dakota with strong southerly warm \nair advection ahead of it across much of our area. This \ncurrently kicking off some elevated showers atop a well mixed \nlayer with about 200mb of dry air beneath them preventing \nanything from reaching the ground. Wouldn't be surprised to see \nsome strong winds occasionally make to the surface as showers \nmove past (30-40mph) via virga bombs and even some lightning \nlater today as the instability profiles builds to over 500 \nMUCAPE. Mixing will remain strongest in out south this afternoon\nwith winds to 20mph and gusts occasionally over 30mph (synoptic\nnot convective). RH has already fallen to near 30 percent along\nthe SD/ND/MN border with further falls into the mid/lower 20s \npercent by 3pm bringing near critical fire weather to southeast \nND and west central MN this afternoon. Fuels remain questionable\nwith ERC still below the daily average (around 15-20). An SPS \nremains in effect to aid the messaging of this but a RFW is not \nbeing considered.\n\n- Weekend rain\n\nTransient vorticity advection through the next 2 days will \nbring scattered showers with a moistening airmass in the wake of\ntodays cold front. Warm air advection will also be a primary \nsource of lift through the period but overall forcing remain \nfairly nebulous through Sunday morning. Thereafter a general \ntenth to quarter inch seems likley along and north of HWY 200 \nfrom Sunday afternoon through Monday night.\n\n- Late week precip chances\n\nPNW troughing by Tuesday will have developed into an upper low \ncompletely cutoff from steering flow over the great basin with a\nstrong jet stream extending northeast from the desert southwest\ninto the midwest and upper great lakes. This strong 100+ kt jet\nstream will induce a strong surface response with moisture \nstreaming northward from the gulf coast as SW flow aloft bring \nforcing later in the week from ejecting shortwaves to ring out \nthe available moisture. The parts that remain uncertain are 1) \nthe northward extent of moisture and 2) how any passing waves \ninteract with said moisture (track/timing). Given this \nuncertainty there remains a decent amount of spread in clusters \nwith anywhere from a 0.25 to over an inch of widespread precip \nfrom Wednesday through Friday next week. Some guidance does show\nan airmass potentially cold enough to bring varying p-types \ninto play but being 5-7 days out still would like to some some \nconsistency in that before raising any cause for alarm.",
